        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedVictor"
      },
      "content": "Three points. First, voting is not a mere privilege we may decline like a dinner invitation; it is the discharge of a civic duty, and a democracy that asks nothing of its citizens is a democracy they will eventually ask nothing of. Second, compulsion cures the free-rider problem: those who abstain still enjoy the benefits of governance, and the burden of a functioning republic should not fall to the willing few. Third, and this is the crux Mei will have to answer, define your terms: compulsory voting does not compel a vote, it compels attendance, and the ballot remains secret and free.",
