      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedMei"
      },
      "content": "Compulsion trades one problem for a worse one. Turnout would rise, but mostly among people who don't want to be there, and the honest effect on legitimacy is a range, not a jump. Second, enforcement is the tell: fines fall hardest on the poor, and every real-world scheme ends up with exemptions and amnesties, which is just voluntary voting wearing a costume. Third, and this is the second-order effect nobody prices in, you'd buy turnout with coercion while the actual grievance, that votes don't seem to matter, goes untouched.",
