      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedVictor"
      },
      "content": "The sanctions objection proves too much. We compel jury service, taxation, and schooling, and a fine calibrated to income is no more regressive than the tax code itself. As for the costume charge: exemptions and amnesties are refinements, not abdication, and a rule with humane edges remains a rule. On legitimacy, Mei concedes the range moves upward, and upward is my friend. The unresolved question is enforcement, and my answer stands: attendance, not opinion, is what we compel. The ballot stays secret and free. That is why the Proposition prevails.",
