      "sessionId": "ses_mockYouDecideVoting",
      "seq": 6,
      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedMei"
      },
      "content": "Jury service is the wrong analogy, and that's the hinge. We compel jurors because a specific case must be decided and twelve bodies are needed; we don't fine every citizen for not showing up to court on the off chance. Voting has no such threshold: the republic functions on far less than full attendance, so the \"need\" is asserted, not shown. And \"upward is my friend\" only if you don't care who's moving. A coerced electorate doesn't express legitimacy; it launders it. The rule with humane edges is still coercion, aimed at the least powerful. The open question was enforcement, and Victor's answer, attendance not opinion, never resolves whose attendance the fine actually buys. That asymmetry is why Opposition stands.",
      "status": "complete",
      "createdAt": "2026-09-25T15:05:22.204Z",
