        "knowledge": {
          "query": "This house would make voting compulsory Victor Hale: The sanctions objection proves too much. We compel jury service, t…",
          "trigger": "gated",
          "retrieved": [
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_meiPilot03",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedMei2",
              "title": "Four-day week pilot summaries",
              "locator": "p. 2",
              "text": "## 1. Who took part\n\nSeventy-one percent of participating organisations were in professional services, software, marketing or the non-profit sector. Only nine provided round-the-clock or shift-based services.\n\n| Sector | Organisations | Workers |\n|------------------------|-----------------|-----------|\n| Professional services | 58 | 2,140 |\n| Software and digital | 49 | 2,310 |\n| Marketing and media | 21 | 640 |\n| Non-profit | 23 | 910 |\n| Manufacturing | 17 | 1,480 |\n| Retail and hospitality | 35 | 1,620 |\n| Shift-based services | 9 | 700 |\n| Total | 212 | 9,800 |\n\nMost organisations had fewer than fifty employees. Only six had more than five hundred.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_meiPilot08",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedMei2",
              "title": "Four-day week pilot summaries",
              "locator": "p. 7",
              "text": "## 6. After the pilots\n\nNinety-two percent of organisations kept the four-day week after the pilot. Participants were not randomly selected, so the authors warn against reading this as a national estimate.\n\nOrganisations that volunteer for a four-day week pilot are likely to be the ones best placed to make it work. A firm that expects it to fail does not sign up.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_meiPilot10",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedMei2",
              "title": "Four-day week pilot summaries",
              "locator": "p. 8",
              "text": "## 7. Recommendations\n\nThe authors recommend sector-specific trials in health, transport and care, with independent measurement of output and service quality, before any national standard is set.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_meiPilot12",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedMei2",
              "title": "Four-day week pilot summaries",
              "locator": "p. 9",
              "text": "## 8. Method and limitations\n\nResults come from employee surveys at the start, midpoint and end of each pilot, alongside finance data reported by each organisation. Survey response fell from 74% to 58% by the final wave.\n\nFalling response rates are a common problem in workplace trials. Workers who were unhappy with the pilot may have been less likely to answer the final survey, which would make results look better than they were.\n\nFinance data was reported by the organisations themselves and was not audited. Output measures differed between firms; fewer than half used one consistent measure for the whole pilot.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_meiPilot06",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedMei2",
              "title": "Four-day week pilot summaries",
              "locator": "p. 5",
              "text": "## 4. The gap in the evidence\n\nNone of the pilots included an acute hospital, a public transport operator or a residential care provider. The authors flag this as the main gap in the evidence.\n\nThese sectors share a feature that most pilot firms did not: service must be provided at fixed times, whatever happens to productivity. A software team can finish a task faster; a bus still has to leave at 7:42.\n\nThe pilots therefore tell us a great deal about knowledge work, and much less about the sectors where a national standard would be hardest to apply.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            },
            {
              "chunkId": "kch_meiPilot11",
              "sourceId": "kno_seedMei2",
              "title": "Four-day week pilot summaries",
              "locator": "p. 8",
              "text": "## In particular:\n\n1. Run trials where the gap is. Fund at least one trial each in an acute hospital trust, a public transport operator and a group of residential care homes.\n2. Measure output independently. Self-reported productivity is not enough. Use service-level data: waiting times, punctuality, care-quality inspections.\n3. Count both sides of the ledger. Record the cost of extra staffed hours and savings from reduced sickness, agency use and turnover.\n4. Randomise where possible. Assign wards, depots or homes to start dates at random, so that results can be compared fairly.\n5. Publish everything, including trials that are stopped early.",
              "score": 0,
              "cited": false
            }
          ]
        },
